<ContentPage
  title="Student Finance"
  description="A plain-English guide to Student Finance England: tuition fee loans, maintenance loans, who can apply and how repayment works."
  eyebrow="Student Finance"
  heading="How to pay for university"
  intro="Most UK students don't pay anything upfront. Here's how Student Finance England works, in plain English."
  ctaHeading="Need help with your application?"
  ctaText="We can walk you through applying for student finance step by step. It's free."
>
  <section>
    <h2>What is Student Finance England?</h2>
    <p>Student Finance England is the government service that provides loans and grants to students from England. It pays your tuition fees directly to your university and can also give you money to help with living costs.</p>
    <p>If you live in Scotland, Wales or Northern Ireland, you apply to SAAS, Student Finance Wales or Student Finance NI instead. The rules are similar but the amounts differ.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Who can apply?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>UK nationals, or people with settled or pre-settled status, who have lived in the UK for the 3 years before the course starts.</li>
      <li>Students studying a first degree, HNC, HND, Foundation Degree or top-up degree at an eligible university or college.</li>
      <li>Mature students. There's no upper age limit for tuition fee loans.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you already have a degree, you usually can't get another tuition fee loan for the same level. There are exceptions, such as nursing, midwifery and some other healthcare courses.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What you can get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tuition fee loan:</strong> covers your course fees, up to the government cap, and is paid straight to your university.</li>
      <li><strong>Maintenance loan:</strong> helps with rent, food and travel. The amount depends on your household income and where you live and study, with more available in London.</li>
      <li><strong>Extra support:</strong> grants for students with children or adult dependants, and Disabled Students' Allowance if you have a disability, long-term health condition or learning difference.</li>
      <li><strong>Healthcare students:</strong> nursing, midwifery and many allied health students can also get a non-repayable grant from the NHS Learning Support Fund.</li>
      <li><strong>Master's students:</strong> a Postgraduate Master's Loan helps with fees and living costs.</li>
      <li><strong>Level 3 courses:</strong> if you're 19 or over, an Advanced Learner Loan can cover course fees.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>From 2027 the Lifelong Learning Entitlement is gradually replacing some of these loans, with more flexible funding for courses and modules. We'll explain what applies to you.</p>
    <p>Amounts change each year. Check the latest figures on <a href="https://www.gov.uk/student-finance" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GOV.UK</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to apply</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Apply online through your student finance account on GOV.UK.</li>
      <li>You don't need a confirmed place. Apply as soon as you've applied to university.</li>
      <li>Apply early, ideally by spring for a September start, so your money is ready when the course begins.</li>
      <li>Have your passport, bank details and National Insurance number ready. If you're applying for a maintenance loan, you may need your household income details too.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How repayment works</h2>
    <p>You only start repaying once you've left your course and earn over the repayment threshold. Repayments are taken automatically from your pay, as a percentage of what you earn above the threshold, not of what you owe.</p>
    <p>If your income drops below the threshold, repayments stop. Any amount left after the write-off period is cancelled. It works more like a graduate tax than a normal loan.</p>
  </section>
</ContentPage>
